  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Impulse Response as a System Description",
    "lesson_core_move": "Use the impulse response $h(t)$ as a complete LTI-system description by mapping shifted and scaled impulse components to corresponding shifted and scaled copies of $h(t)$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Impulse Response as a System Description",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Impulse Response as a System Description",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For an LTI system, the impulse response $h(t)$ is more than one output expression. It is the response template for the system: once you know what the system does to the unit impulse, you can reuse that response for related impulse inputs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The base case is the unshifted unit impulse. By definition, when the input is $\\delta(t)$, the output is $h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t) \\to h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Time invariance says that moving the impulse in time moves the response by the same amount. So a delayed impulse $\\delta(t - T)$ produces the delayed response $h(t - T)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t - T) \\to h(t - T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Linearity says that scaling the impulse scales the whole response by the same constant. So an input $a\\delta(t)$ produces the output $a h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a\\delta(t) \\to a h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Putting both properties together gives the reusable mapping for one impulse component: copy the impulse's coefficient and time shift onto $h(t)$. This is the local mechanism behind later impulse decomposition and convolution, but here the move is only this single-component mapping.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a\\delta(t - T) \\to a h(t - T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Impulse Response as a System Description",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Reading $h(t)$ as the Response Template for the Unit Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has impulse response $h(t) = e^{-2t}u(t)$. What is the output when the input is $\\delta(t)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the input $\\delta(t)$. This is the unshifted, unscaled unit impulse, so we use the base impulse-response mapping.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t) \\to h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The problem already gives the system's impulse response as $h(t) = e^{-2t}u(t)$. Since the input is exactly $\\delta(t)$, the output is exactly that same function.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t) = h(t) = e^{-2t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output is $e^{-2t}u(t)$. No time shift or amplitude scale has been applied yet; this is the response template before any modification.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Impulse Response as a System Description",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Mapping a Time-Shifted Impulse Component to a Time-Shifted Copy of $h(t)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has impulse response $h(t) = e^{-t}u(t)$. What is the output when the input is $\\delta(t - 4)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the input $\\delta(t - 4)$. This is a unit impulse delayed by 4, with no amplitude scale, so only the time-invariance part of the response-template rule is active.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t - 4) \\to h(t - 4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Time invariance says the output receives the same delay as the impulse input. The system output is not another impulse; it is the impulse response template shifted by the same amount.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t) = h(t - 4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute $t - 4$ wherever $t$ appears in the given formula $h(t) = e^{-t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t) = h(t - 4) = e^{-(t - 4)}u(t - 4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output is $e^{-(t - 4)}u(t - 4)$. It is the same response shape as $h(t)$, delayed by 4.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Impulse Response as a System Description",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Mapping an Amplitude-Scaled Impulse Component to an Amplitude-Scaled Copy of $h(t)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has impulse response $h(t) = e^{-3t}u(t)$. What is the output when the input is $5\\delta(t)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the input $5\\delta(t)$. This is an amplitude-scaled unit impulse at $t = 0$, so there is no time shift to apply.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use linearity: scaling the impulse input by 5 scales the entire impulse-response output by 5.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "5\\delta(t) \\to 5h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now multiply the given impulse response by 5. The multiplier changes the response amplitude; it does not change the time variable inside $h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t) = 5h(t) = 5e^{-3t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output is $5e^{-3t}u(t)$. It is an amplitude-scaled copy of $h(t)$ with no delay or advance.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Impulse Response as a System Description",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Mapping a Scaled and Shifted Impulse Component to a Scaled and Shifted Copy of $h(t)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has impulse response $h(t) = e^{-t}u(t)$. What is the output when the input is $3\\delta(t - 2)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the input $3\\delta(t - 2)$. It is one impulse component with two pieces to copy: the scalar coefficient $3$ and the delay $2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For an LTI system, time invariance copies the delay onto $h(t)$, and linearity copies the scalar onto the whole shifted response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a\\delta(t - T) \\to a h(t - T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here, $a = 3$ and $T = 2$. So $\\delta(t - 2)$ becomes $h(t - 2)$, and the multiplier $3$ stays attached to that shifted response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "3\\delta(t - 2) \\to 3h(t - 2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute $t - 2$ into the given impulse response $h(t) = e^{-t}u(t)$, then keep the outside multiplier $3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t) = 3h(t - 2) = 3e^{-(t - 2)}u(t - 2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output is $3e^{-(t - 2)}u(t - 2)$. It is one scaled and delayed copy of the response template $h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
